\section{The constructor}{

A constructor is a function of \verb{(config, weights)} returning a
\code{\link[=new_zuk_model]{new_zuk_model()}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{config} — a named list parsed from the checkpoint's \code{config.json}, with
\code{architecture}, \code{model_id}, and \code{revision} filled in by the loader.
\item \code{weights} — a state dict, or \code{NULL} for weight-free architectures.
}

The constructor must not perform inference, and must not download anything
beyond what the loader already resolved.
}

\section{The forward pass}{

Every architecture supplies \code{predict_fn}, and may additionally supply
\code{predict_batch_fn} for true vectorised inference.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{predict_fn(context, h, quantile_levels) -> matrix[h, q]
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
\itemize{
\item \code{context} — a numeric vector of observed history, \strong{oldest first}, already
truncated to \code{max_context} and with \code{NA} removed by \code{\link[=zuk_run_batches]{zuk_run_batches()}}.
\item \code{h} — a positive integer horizon.
\item \code{quantile_levels} — a sorted numeric vector in \verb{(0, 1)}.
\item Returns a numeric matrix with \code{h} rows and \code{length(quantile_levels)}
columns. Column \code{j} holds the forecasts at \code{quantile_levels[j]}.
}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{predict_batch_fn(contexts, horizons, quantile_levels, device) -> list(matrix)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
\itemize{
\item \code{contexts} — a list of numeric context vectors.
\item \code{horizons} — an integer vector of per-series horizons, aligned to
\code{contexts}.
\item \code{device} — a resolved device string (never \code{"auto"}); see
\code{\link[=zuk_resolve_device]{zuk_resolve_device()}}.
\item Returns a list of quantile matrices, aligned to and the same length as
\code{contexts}.
}

When \code{predict_batch_fn} is \code{NULL}, \code{\link[=zuk_run_batches]{zuk_run_batches()}} loops \code{predict_fn}.
When both are present they must agree: the batch path is an optimisation,
never a different model.
